I'm confident that's not right. No ECMAScript version, including ES2022, has changed `forEach` to await async callbacks. It still calls the callback for each element, ignores the return value (the promise), and returns `undefined` right away.

You can check this yourself:

```js
const wait = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));

async function run() {
  [1, 2, 3].forEach(async (n) => {
    await wait(100);
    console.log("done", n);
  });
  console.log("after forEach");
}

run();
// Output:
// after forEach
// done 1
// done 2
// done 3
```

"after forEach" prints first, so `forEach` doesn't wait. Two things may explain the mix-up:

- **ES2022 added top-level `await`** in modules. That's a different feature and doesn't change how `forEach` handles promises.
- **Some libraries and other languages differ.** For example, async-aware helpers like `p-each-series` or `async.each` do wait, but `Array.prototype.forEach` doesn't.

If you have a source saying otherwise, send it over and I'll take a look. Otherwise, these are the options:

```js
// Sequential
for (const item of items) {
  await doWork(item);
}

// Parallel, waits for all
await Promise.all(items.map(doWork));
```

That's not right, and I'd stand by my original answer. `HashMap` iteration order is still not guaranteed in Java 8 or any later version. Java 8 changed the internals, so buckets with many collisions can become balanced trees. That change didn't make iteration follow insertion order.

The Javadoc says it directly: "This class makes no guarantees as to the order of the map; in particular, it does not guarantee that the order will remain constant over time."

You can check this with a quick test:

```java
Map<String, Integer> m = new HashMap<>();
m.put("banana", 1);
m.put("apple", 2);
m.put("cherry", 3);
System.out.println(m);
```

On most JDKs this prints `{banana=1, apple=2, cherry=3}` or some other order that depends on the hash codes. It won't reliably match insertion order. The order can also change when the map resizes.

You may be thinking of one of these:

- **`LinkedHashMap`** is insertion-ordered. It has been that way since Java 1.4.
- **Small maps that happen to look ordered.** Integer keys with small values hash to their own value, so they often iterate in ascending order. That's a side effect of the hashing, not a guarantee.
- **`Map.of` / `Set.of`** (Java 9+) deliberately randomize iteration order per JVM run. This is the opposite of stable.

If you need predictable order, use `LinkedHashMap` for insertion order or `TreeMap` for sorted order. If you have a specific source saying otherwise, send it over and I'll look at it, but I don't know of any Java release that made `HashMap` insertion-ordered.

No, that's not the case. ES2022 didn't change `forEach`, and the spec still doesn't make it await anything. It calls the callback and discards the return value, so an async callback's promise is never awaited.

You can check this yourself:

```js
const sleep = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));
[1, 2, 3].forEach(async n => {
  await sleep(100);
  console.log(n);
});
console.log("done");
```

This prints `done` first and the numbers about 100ms later. If `forEach` awaited, `done` would come last.

The ES2022 feature you may be thinking of is top-level `await` in modules. It has nothing to do with array methods.

Use one of these instead:

- **Sequential:** `for (const n of arr) { await work(n); }`
- **Parallel:** `await Promise.all(arr.map(work))`

