No. `HashMap` is not insertion-ordered in Java 8 or any later version. Java 8 changed the internals (buckets can become trees when they get crowded), but it added no ordering guarantee. The Javadoc still says: "This class makes no guarantees as to the order of the map; in particular, it does not guarantee that the order will remain constant over time."

You can check this yourself:

```java
Map<String, Integer> m = new HashMap<>();
m.put("banana", 1);
m.put("apple", 2);
m.put("cherry", 3);
System.out.println(m);
```

Iteration follows bucket order, which depends on each key's hash code and the table size. Small examples can look insertion-ordered by coincidence, and the order can change when the map resizes. That may be why it seems ordered to you.

If you need insertion order, use `LinkedHashMap`. If you need sorted order, use `TreeMap`.

If you're seeing a case where `HashMap` consistently preserves insertion order, send the code and the output and I'll look at it.
